            <ArticleTitle>A study in familial hypercholesterolemia suggests reduced methylomic plasticity in men with coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>17-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/epi.14.64</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess whether DNA methylation is associated with coronary artery disease (CAD).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">An epigenome-wide analysis has been performed on leucocytes from familial hypercholesterolemic (FH) men with (n = 6) or without CAD (n = 6). The results were replicated in an extended sample of FH men (n = 61) and in non-FH men (n = 100) for two of the top differentially methylated loci.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">FH men with CAD had significantly more hypomethylated and hypermethylated loci and showed less DNA methylation level variability compared with men without CAD (p &lt; 0.001). Moreover, COL14A1 and MMP9 DNA methylation levels were associated with CAD, age of onset of CAD or CAD risk factors.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">These results suggest that epigenome-wide changes are associated with CAD occurrence in men.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
